{{!
    @template theme_nit/core/signup_form_layout

    Overrides core/signup_form_layout to build the screen §4.1 describes.

    Core renders a bare heading and the form. The specification's screen-elements
    table also lists a supporting line under the heading, "Sign in with Google" and
    "Sign in with Apple" buttons, and the "Already have an account? Log in" link -
    none of which core's sign-up page has any concept of. The providers arrive from
    theme_nit\output\core_renderer::render_login_signup_form().

    The field layout itself (two-column names, the inline reveal toggle, the
    strength meter) is styled in scss/components/_authpages.scss rather than
    rebuilt here: the boxes are rendered by moodleform, and restyling its output is
    what keeps this working across Moodle upgrades.

    Context variables required for this template:
    * logourl - the site logo, or false
    * sitename - the site's full name
    * formhtml - the rendered sign-up form
    * nithasproviders - whether any identity provider is configured
    * nitidentityproviders - [{url, iconurl, name}]
    * languagemenu - the language switcher, as core/action_menu context, or empty
    * canloginasguest - whether the site offers guest access
    * loginurl - where the guest form POSTs (login/index.php)
    * logintoken - the session's login token, for that POST

    Example context (json):
    {
        "logourl": "https://moodle.org/logo/moodle-logo.svg",
        "sitename": "EAAC",
        "formhtml": "<p>(Form html would go here)</p>",
        "nithasproviders": true,
        "nitidentityproviders": [
            {"url": "https://localhost/auth/oauth2/login.php", "iconurl": "", "name": "Google"}
        ],
        "languagemenu": false,
        "canloginasguest": true,
        "loginurl": "https://localhost/login/index.php",
        "logintoken": "abc123"
    }
}}
{{!
    `nit-auth-card` is the same card the log-in screen sits in. The two screens
    are one journey and a learner crosses between them by a link at the bottom of
    each; without it, sign-up read as a bare form floating on the page while
    log-in read as a card, which is two products rather than two steps.
}}
<div class="signupform nit-auth nit-auth-card">
    <div class="nit-auth-head">
        <h1 class="nit-auth-title">{{#str}} createaccount, theme_nit {{/str}}</h1>
        <p class="nit-auth-sub">{{#str}} createaccountsub, theme_nit {{/str}}</p>
    </div>

    {{{formhtml}}}

    {{#nithasproviders}}
        <div class="nit-auth-divider" role="separator">
            <span>{{#str}} or, theme_nit {{/str}}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="nit-auth-providers">
            {{#nitidentityproviders}}
                <a class="btn nit-auth-provider" href="{{url}}">
                    {{#iconurl}}
                        <img src="{{iconurl}}" alt="" width="20" height="20">
                    {{/iconurl}}
                    <span>{{#str}} continuewith, theme_nit, {{name}} {{/str}}</span>
                </a>
            {{/nitidentityproviders}}
        </div>
    {{/nithasproviders}}

    <p class="nit-auth-foot">
        <span>{{#str}} alreadyhaveaccount, theme_nit {{/str}}</span>
        <a href="{{config.wwwroot}}/login/index.php">{{#str}} logintoaccount, theme_nit {{/str}}</a>
    </p>

    {{!
        The same utility line the log-in card ends with: the language switcher
        and guest access. Sign-up and log-in are one journey, and a visitor who
        arrived here in the wrong language should not have to cross back to
        log-in to change it. Markup and classes are identical to
        theme_nit/core/loginform so the two screens keep one look and one
        stylesheet rule (`.nit-auth-utility` in _authpages.scss).
    }}
    <div class="nit-auth-utility">
        {{#languagemenu}}
            <div class="login-languagemenu">
                {{>core/action_menu}}
            </div>
        {{/languagemenu}}
        {{#canloginasguest}}
            <form action="{{loginurl}}" method="post" id="guestlogin" class="nit-auth-guest">
                <input type="hidden" name="logintoken" value="{{logintoken}}">
                <input type="hidden" name="username" value="guest" />
                <input type="hidden" name="password" value="guest" />
                <button class="btn nit-auth-utility-btn" type="submit" id="loginguestbtn">{{!
                    !}}{{#str}}loginguest{{/str}}</button>
            </form>
        {{/canloginasguest}}
    </div>
</div>
